 # Standard Multi-resolution Hash Encoding
 
-Also called: Instant-NGP Hash Encoding, Müller et al. Hash Encoding
 
 ## Overview
-Standard Multi-resolution Hash Encoding maps 3D spatial coordinates to a high-dimensional feature vector using a set of trainable, sparse hash grids at multiple resolutions, enabling efficient neural rendering by replacing expensive positional encoding and large MLPs with a compact, trainable representation [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of slow training times and high memory consumption associated with traditional NeRF models by allowing the encoding itself to be trained alongside a shallow network, achieving faster convergence and reduced parameter counts [S1, unofficial]. Unlike its siblings, it relies on a fixed-size hash table structure with trilinear interpolation and a specific hash function, whereas alternatives may use simplex structures, tetrahedron querying, or global preconditioners.
+Standard Multi-resolution Hash Encoding maps 3D spatial coordinates to a high-dimensional feature vector using a set of trainable, fixed-size hash grids at multiple resolutions, enabling efficient neural rendering by replacing expensive positional encoding and large MLPs with a compact, trainable representation [S2, S3]. It solves the problem of slow training times and high memory consumption associated with traditional NeRF models by allowing the encoding itself to be trained alongside a shallow network, achieving faster convergence and reduced parameter counts [S2, S3]. Unlike its siblings, it relies on a fixed-size hash table structure with trilinear interpolation and a specific hash function, whereas alternatives may use simplex structures, tetrahedron querying, or global preconditioners [G].
 
