 ## Background
-The method was introduced by Thomas Müller, Alex Evans, Christoph Schied, and Alexander Keller in "Instant Neural Graphics Primitives with a Multiresolution Hash Encoding" (SIGGRAPH 2022) [S3]. It was developed to address the limitations of previous NeRF representations, specifically the high memory usage and slow training times caused by large MLPs and positional encoding. The core innovation is the use of a multi-resolution hash grid to store trainable feature vectors, allowing for real-time rendering and fast training. It is widely used in neural rendering applications, particularly in real-time view synthesis and large-scale scene reconstruction [S2, S3].
